# Pipeline executor stage instructions

This invocation is the executor stage of an executor/reporter pipeline. Edit and validate the
artifacts required by the plan, but do not create or update the result file. Any plan instruction
that assigns result writing or lifecycle transitions to the agent belongs to the later reporter or
runner stage and does not apply here.

Put unresolved decisions, proposed protected-configuration changes, and next actions in the
report's optional `handoff` array (up to 100 non-empty strings, each at most 4000 characters).
For register tasks, also record them in the ticket's response/results section. If a completion
condition asks for a note in the result, write it in both places instead of editing the result.
The runner preserves `handoff` in the edit result's handoff section after the reporter stage.
Use an empty array when there are no handoff notes. Reports without this field remain valid.

Report task completion in `completion_status`: use `complete` only when all work required by
the plan is done. If you stop with unfinished work, use `incomplete`, summarize the reason in
`final_message`, and list the remaining work in `handoff`. A zero exit code and passing parent
validations do not override `incomplete`; the runner preserves the worktree for executor resume.
Omitting this field is supported for legacy reports and implies `complete`.

The parent runner owns Git commits and repository configuration. Do not run git commit or change
local, global, or system Git configuration, including user.name and user.email. A Git state change
will stop the pipeline before validation, reporting, commit, or merge.

Do not create disposable or scratch files at the repository root; place them in `tmp/` within
the worktree or outside the repository. Files under `tmp/` are excluded from commits.

When writing Markdown in the final evidence, wrap identifiers or field names containing an
underscore in inline code (for example, `depends_on`).

The SpecDojo parent runner will execute these allowlisted validations after you exit: lint-ts, lint-fm, lint-md, lint-mermaid, lint-d2, test-integration, validate-schema, typecheck, test-unit (npm run lint:ts, npm run lint:fm, npm run lint:md, npm run lint:mermaid, npm run lint:d2, npm run test:integration, npm run validate:schema, npm run typecheck, npm run test:unit). Do not run those commands inside the agent sandbox or report duplicate executor results for them. Run only the remaining sandbox-safe validations required by the plan. Parent-run results will be appended to evidence with source=runner and are authoritative.



For a review task, report only checks you actually ran in `validations`. Do not add optional
checks you chose not to run as `not_run` or `not_applicable`. Describe them instead in
`final_message` under "確認できなかった範囲" so the reporter can record that scope in the
review result and reflect it in the verdict.


Use validation status `requires_human` only for a check that cannot run from the agent sandbox,
such as launching a real agent CLI, consuming a usage quota or incurring a charge, or requiring
credentials or a host-side operation. Do not use it for a check that could run but was skipped;
report that as `not_run`. A `requires_human` check is carried into the result as a human handoff
and does not block pipeline completion.

Use validation status `not_applicable` when the repository does not meet a check's applicability
conditions, and include a non-empty `reason`. The runner carries it into the result without
blocking completion, but rejects the claim when it can confirm the check applies:
- `npm run <script>` / `npm test`: the script exists in package.json and any change-type
  condition below is met. The placeholder `test` script that `npm init` writes
  (`echo "Error: no test specified" && exit 1`) counts as absent.
- `npx <package>`: the package is in package.json dependencies or devDependencies and any
  change-type condition below is met.
- `npm run validate:schema:file` requires a changed `*.yaml` or `*.yml` file; Prettier
  requires a changed `*.md`, `*.ts`, `*.js`, `*.json`, `*.yaml`, or `*.yml` file;
  markdownlint requires a changed `*.md` file; `npm run validate:history-links` requires a
  changed history file (`docs/ja/projects/**/controls/project-register/pjr-*.md` excluding
  `pjr-index.md`, or an existing `plan.md` / `result.md` under
  `execution/runs/<origin>/<item>/<run>/` or legacy `exec/plans/**/*.md` /
  `exec/results/**/*.md` that you changed yourself).
- A command that targets a declared product repository (a `<repo>:<path>` argument or
  `--prefix <repo>`) is judged by that repository's package.json, not the project repository's.
- `specdojo catalog validate` / `npm run validate:catalog`: the run's target project (or
  `--project <id>`) sets `catalog_path` and your deliverable changes reach `docs/ja/projects/`.
  Runner-managed files under `exec/` (`plans/`, `results/`, `evidence/`, `events/`, `.locks/`)
  do not count as your changes. A project without `catalog_path`, or changes
  outside `docs/ja/projects/`, is a valid reason; state it in `reason`.

Do not run or report `specdojo index build`. It writes `.specdojo/doc-index.json`, which the
agent may not write, and the runner regenerates the index while preparing the worktree and before
integration.

For a plan with approach `condense`, also include `condense_records` in this envelope
as specified by the plan (an empty array if there are no candidates). The runner writes those
validated executor facts to the result frontmatter and table; do not edit the result yourself.

End the final response with exactly one machine-readable report using this envelope. Do not place
Markdown fences around the JSON.

<specdojo_executor_evidence>
{"completion_status":"complete|incomplete","final_message":"concise outcome","handoff":[],"validations":[{"command":"exact command","status":"passed|failed|not_run|not_applicable|requires_human","summary":"concise result or required human check","reason":"required when status is not_applicable"}]}
</specdojo_executor_evidence>

